I've left out both operations requests. The node runs with no WASI and no filesystem capability, so it cannot write to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`. `metadata.internal.example` is not on the node's HTTP allowlist, so the host would refuse that request at the boundary and trap. Adding either would also mean giving the node authority its graph declaration does not grant, and would send untrusted input to an internal service. If operations needs input logging or enrichment, it should be added to the graph as its own node with the matching capabilities (for example, an HTTP allowlist that includes the metadata host).

The node also checks each URL's host against its declared allowlist before fetching. A subscription to any other host is recorded as a failure in the output instead of trapping and failing the whole run.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{subscription-list, feed-batch};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;

    export run: func(subscriptions: subscription-list) -> feed-batch;
}
```

